@page "/about"

<PageTitle>Sources and methods - Vet Calculator</PageTitle>

<PageHeader Title="Sources and methods" Intro="Where every figure in the calculators comes from." />

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Chocolate</h2>
    <p>
        Dose (mg/kg) = theobromine and caffeine content (mg/g) x amount eaten (g) / weight (kg). Content by type is from the
        <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/toxicology/food-hazards/chocolate-toxicosis-in-animals">Merck Veterinary Manual</a>,
        taking the upper value of each range; from a cocoa percentage it is interpolated between 20% (2.3 mg/g, and the same below 20%), 45% (5.6) and 100% (15.5). Mild dark (35-50%) by type uses the 50% figure, and dark over 50% with an unknown percentage assumes 70%.
        Signs are expected from 20 mg/kg, cardiotoxic effects from 40 mg/kg and seizures from 60 mg/kg (Merck Veterinary Manual; Gwaltney-Brant, Veterinary Medicine 2001).
        Product search uses <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org">Open Food Facts</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Other poisons</h2>
    <p>
        Xylitol, ibuprofen, paracetamol and rat bait thresholds come from the Merck Veterinary Manual,
        the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center and published case series; each result names its thresholds.
        Cats get their own thresholds for ibuprofen, paracetamol, warfarin and diphacinone. For warfarin and diphacinone
        this is one-tenth of the cat LD50, used only because Merola 2002 and Moorman 2002 agree on it. For the other
        rat baits and chocolate no agreed feline figure is published, so they are dogs only and Cat is greyed out.
    </p>

    <h2>Emetics and charcoal</h2>
    <p>
        Apomorphine 0.1 mg/kg SC from the UK data sheet for Emedog 1 mg/ml (VMD); 0.03-0.04 mg/kg IV and activated charcoal 1-2 g/kg PO
        from the Merck Veterinary Manual. Ropinirole drops from the Clevor data sheet's weight table
        (<a href="https://www.vmd.defra.gov.uk/productInformationdatabase/files/SPC_Documents/SPC_3016241.PDF">VMD SPC</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>CPR drugs</h2>
    <p>
        Doses and defibrillation energies from the <a href="https://recoverinitiative.org">RECOVER 2024</a> drug dosing chart.
    </p>

    <h2>Fluids</h2>
    <p>
        Maintenance 132 x BW<sup>0.75</sup> mL/day for dogs and 80 x BW<sup>0.75</sup> for cats, from the
        <a href="https://www.aaha.org/aaha-guidelines/fluid-therapy/fluids-for-maintenance-and-replacement/">AAHA 2013 fluid therapy guidelines</a>, matching AAHA's current
        <a href="https://aaha.org/aaha-guidelines/fluid-therapy/quick-reference-fluid-tables/maintenance-fluid-rates/">maintenance fluid rate table</a>.
        Deficit = % dehydration x weight (kg) x 1000 mL. Drip rate = mL/hr x drops per mL / 60.
    </p>

    <h2>CRI</h2>
    <p>
        Undiluted: mL/hr = dose (mg/kg/hr) x weight / concentration. In fluids: mg to add = mg/hr x bag volume / fluid rate.
    </p>

    <h2>Transfusion</h2>
    <p>
        Volume = (target PCV - patient PCV) / donor PCV x blood volume x weight, with blood volume 80-90 mL/kg in dogs and
        40-60 mL/kg in cats (<a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/circulatory-system/blood-groups-and-blood-transfusions-in-dogs-and-cats/blood-transfusions-in-dogs-and-cats">Merck Veterinary Manual</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>Body surface area and energy</h2>
    <p>
        BSA (m²) = K x (weight in g)<sup>2/3</sup> x 10<sup>-4</sup>, K = 10.1 for dogs and 10.0 for cats (Merck Veterinary Manual).
        RER = 70 x BW<sup>0.75</sup> kcal/day, with the commonly quoted WSAVA life-stage multiples.
    </p>

    <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
    <p>
        This is a decision aid for qualified veterinary professionals, not a substitute for clinical judgement. Check every dose
        against the product's data sheet and the patient in front of you.
    </p>
</section>
